%description:

RFC 9000 -- connection close.

Checks RFC9000-CLOSE-1 (description of the mechanism), from
doc/project/evidence/standard/rfc9000/catalog.md. The English procedure and mockup are in
doc/project/evidence/protocol/quic/checks/close.md#connection-close; the program steps below map one to
one onto its expected observations. Mockup mapping: host A = client, host B = server.

Scenario: examples/quic/basic3's own network and QuicClient/QuicDiscardServer apps, unmodified.
QuicClient *does* close on its own: socketEstablished() (QuicClient.cc) sends its two writes and
then unconditionally calls `socket->close()` in the same handler, with no ini parameter gating it
-- so this check is runnable, and the model's own "immediate" semantics for CONNECTION_CLOSE
(Connection::close() calls sendConnectionClose() synchronously, before anything else) mean the
close is sent essentially back-to-back with the data, not after a delay: consistent with checks.md's
own description of the mechanism ("an endpoint terminates the connection immediately").

Implementation note: as in the other QUIC tests in this suite, evalPacketField()/.packet("quic....")
cannot see inside a QUIC packet; this test finds the CONNECTION_CLOSE and STREAM frames directly
on the packet's own chunk tree via QuicChunks.h's findQuicChunk<T>(). ConnectionCloseFrameHeader
is the one C++ class for both CONNECTION_CLOSE variants (QUIC-layer error, frameType 0x1c, and
application-layer error, frameType 0x1d -- FrameHeader.msg); QuicClient's app-initiated close
produces the app variant (confirmed empirically: `CONNECTION_CLOSE[APP, errorCode=0]`), so this
test does not restrict frameType and accepts either, matching checks.md's mechanism-level wording.

Deviation on the scope of observation 2: checks.md asks for "no datagram carrying a STREAM frame
... in either direction" after the close. This test checks only host A's (the client's) outgoing
side, because the reverse direction is trivially true throughout the *entire* run, not just after
the close: QuicDiscardServer (src/inet/applications/quicapp/QuicDiscardServer.cc) only ever reads
and discards -- it has no code path that originates a STREAM frame -- so asserting "no STREAM frame
from host B" would verify nothing check-specific. Host A's side is the substantive half: it is the
side that was sending data and had to actually stop.

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"
#include "QuicChunks.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("rfc9000_connection_close")
        // Expected observation 1: host A sends a datagram whose QUIC packet carries a
        // CONNECTION_CLOSE frame. Confirms the stimulus: the close really started
        // (RFC9000-CLOSE-1).
        .once(on("client.udp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .match([](const MatchContext& c) {
                      return findQuicChunk<ConnectionCloseFrameHeader>(c.event.packet) != nullptr;
                  })
                  .describe("host A's datagram carrying a CONNECTION_CLOSE frame")
                  .within(0.5))
        // Expected observation 2: from then on, no datagram carrying a STREAM frame
        // travels from host A -- the stream is closed. See the %description for why the
        // server->client half is not separately asserted.
        .never(on("client.udp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .match([](const MatchContext& c) {
                      return findQuicChunk<StreamFrameHeader>(c.event.packet) != nullptr;
                  })
                  .describe("a further STREAM frame from host A")
                  .within(4.0));
}

%activity:
// present only so opp_test compiles the %global above; this module is not in the network.
EV << "rfc9000_connection_close compiled\n";

%file: test.ned

import inet.node.inet.StandardHost;
import inet.node.ethernet.Eth100M;
import inet.networklayer.configurator.ipv4.Ipv4NetworkConfigurator;

// Mockup of doc/project/evidence/protocol/quic/checks.md's "Common mockup":
// host A (client) -- path P -- host B (server).
network QuicNet
{
    submodules:
        configurator: Ipv4NetworkConfigurator { @display("p=100,50"); }
        client: StandardHost { @display("p=250,100"); }
        server: StandardHost { @display("p=450,100"); }
    connections:
        client.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> server.ethg++;
}

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
network = QuicNet
sim-time-limit = 5s
seed-set = 0

# examples/quic/basic3's own client/server apps, unmodified: the client opens a connection,
# writes 1000B then 500B, and closes.
**.client.hasQuic = true
**.client.numApps = 1
**.client.app[*].typename = "QuicClient"
**.client.app[*].localPort = 1001
**.client.app[*].connectPort = 1000
**.client.app[*].connectAddress = "server"

**.server.hasQuic = true
**.server.numApps = 1
**.server.app[*].typename = "QuicDiscardServer"
**.server.app[*].localAddress = ""
**.server.app[*].localPort = 1000

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST rfc9000_connection_close: PASS
